//
//  RadarRamp.swift  (WeatherKit)
//
//  The colour ramp the tile service actually draws with, recovered from its own
//  legend, so the app can read INTENSITY back out of a rendered pixel.
//
//  WHY THIS EXISTS. The WMS returns RGBA with every non-empty pixel at alpha
//  255 — a drizzle pixel is exactly as opaque as a thunderstorm core. Drawn at a
//  flat 0.75 the result is a solid poster-paint smear with no depth, which is
//  what made the radar look amateurish. To vary opacity BY intensity, something
//  has to know which colours are weak returns and which are strong, and the
//  service offers no way to ask: `GetFeatureInfo` is not enabled on this layer
//  and `GetLegendGraphic` returns a 35x5 stub.
//
//  It does publish a legend bar at /GIS/legends/N0Q.gif — 240 colour steps in
//  intensity order — and that is where this table comes from, read top to bottom
//  and normalised 0...1.
//
//  DELIBERATELY NOT dBZ. Calibrating the bar to absolute reflectivity needs the
//  tick labels, which are text in a GIF, and every guess at them put ordinary
//  light rain at 40+ dBZ — a number that would then be wrong on a legend and in
//  any sentence built from it. The ORDER is certain because it is the ramp
//  itself; the absolute scale is not. So this reports position in the ramp and
//  the UI speaks in weak/moderate/heavy, which is what the rendering needs and
//  all that can be honestly claimed.
//

